d joining methods, forming, tolerances, material and thickness selection, and how design choices affect tooling, cost, assembly and quality.</span></p></li></ul><p style="min-height: 1.7em;"></p><ul><li><p class="paragraph"><strong><span style="color:#121317">CAD &amp; Drawings</span></strong><span style="color:#121317">: You are an expert SolidWorks user and remain hands-on with CAD. You personally create high-quality manufacturing drawings and are confident with GD&amp;T and tolerance chains.</span></p></li></ul><p style="min-height: 1.7em;"></p><ul><li><p class="paragraph"><strong><span style="color:#121317">System Design &amp; Engineering Judgement: </span></strong><span style="color:#121317">You have experience designing from components and mechanisms through subsystems, interfaces and complex assemblies. You understand how decisions at one level affect the complete collector and can balance structures, airflow, movement, sealing, process interfaces, manufacturability, reliability and cost to arrive at robust solutions.</span></p></li></ul><p style="min-height: 1.7em;"></p><ul><li><p class="paragraph"><strong><span style="color:#121317">Manufacturing &amp; DFMA</span></strong><span style="color:#121317">: You have strong knowledge of machined parts, welded structures, materials and manufacturing processes, and apply DFMA to create robust, cost-effective designs.</span></p></li></ul><p style="min-height: 1.7em;"></p><ul><li><p class="paragraph"><strong><span style="color:#121317">Collaboration &amp; Technical Leadership</span></strong><span style="color:#121317">: You work effectively with engineers, technicians, manufacturers and suppliers. You share your experience, challenge constructively and help others make better technical decisions.</span></p></li></ul><p style="min-height: 1.7em;"></p><ul><li><p class="paragraph"><strong><span style="color:#121317">Education:</span></strong><span style="color:#121317"> You have a Bachelor’s or Master’s degree in Mechani